topics.body:
The credit memo must show the Applicant's **ability and likelihood to repay from business cash flow and past performance**. The SOP lists what it must document.

#### 1. Business description and history

- Nature of the business, **including the NAICS code** (critical for MARC, since the industry drives eligibility and the loan maximum);
- Length of time in business **under current management**;
- Depth of management experience in the industry or a related one;
- A brief description of the management team, including the principals' involvement in **daily onsite management** or how operations are run if they are not there daily;
- If daily operations run under a **management agreement**, the Lender must **obtain a copy**, **review it** to determine whether it results in an **ineligible passive business**, and **retain it** in the loan file. The only exception is a management agreement that is part of the franchise disclosure documents for a brand on the **Franchise Directory**.

#### 2. Financial analysis of repayment ability

Repayment is analyzed under **13 CFR 120.191**, and the analysis must show cash flow can cover **all debt obligations, including the proposed SBA loan, on a fully amortizing basis using the maximum loan amount and maturity approved**.

For every Applicant, the cash flow analysis includes:

- **EBITDA**;
- Justification for adjustments such as unfunded capex, non-recurring income or expenses, expenses and distributions, S-Corp tax distributions, rent, and owner's draw;
- A **global cash flow** analysis covering cash flow to and from affiliates (anticipated rental income from the Project Property may be included), and the effect affiliates may have on repayment.

For **projections**, the Lender must calculate DSC and support the assumptions: revenue growth (new products, sales channels, production facilities), expense reductions, and a comparison to industry trends. It also needs a **pro forma balance sheet**, ratio calculations (Current Ratio, Debt/Tangible Net Worth, DSC, and other relevant ratios such as inventory, receivables and payables turnover) compared to industry trends, and an analysis of **working capital adequacy**.

#### 3. Collateral, insurance and rationale

- **Collateral adequacy** under **Appendix 19, Para. B.4**;
- **Insurance:** life insurance (on whom and how much, or a justification for not requiring it), plus business hazard and liability insurance;
- **Rationale for approval**, discussing: credit not available elsewhere from non-Federal, non-State, non-local government sources (**Section A, Ch. 1, Para. H**); competition; seller financing; standby agreements; 90+ day delinquencies; trade disputes; government citations that would preclude normal operations; liens, judgments, bankruptcies or pending litigation (including divorce); for an FTC-defined **franchise**, credit information such as failed franchisees and franchisor projections; and other relevant information.
